[SPEAKER_01]: Wouldn't you want to get out?

211
00:10:54,970 --> 00:10:57,152
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it was quite a lot.

212
00:10:57,172 --> 00:10:59,755
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you have much time to play ball when you worked in the last plant?

213
00:10:59,955 --> 00:11:00,696
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh yeah, yeah.

214
00:11:00,716 --> 00:11:01,437
[SPEAKER_00]: We played well.

215
00:11:01,477 --> 00:11:03,198
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, we played every Saturday and Sunday.

216
00:11:03,939 --> 00:11:04,240
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

217
00:11:04,983 --> 00:11:06,984
[SPEAKER_01]: This was a city league in Muncie?

218
00:11:07,024 --> 00:11:07,844
[SPEAKER_01]: It was a city league.

219
00:11:07,864 --> 00:11:09,445
[SPEAKER_00]: It was an organized league you were playing?

220
00:11:09,465 --> 00:11:11,506
[SPEAKER_00]: No, just city league.

221
00:11:11,546 --> 00:11:14,868
[SPEAKER_00]: Factory boys would get together and form teams.

222
00:11:15,448 --> 00:11:16,689
[SPEAKER_00]: We'd play among ourselves.

223
00:11:17,609 --> 00:11:26,214
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, of course, after I got to playing pretty good, they started wanting me in these little towns around here.

224
00:11:26,354 --> 00:11:27,034
[SPEAKER_00]: Reputation.

225
00:11:27,054 --> 00:11:27,154
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

226
00:11:27,194 --> 00:11:30,556
[SPEAKER_01]: You were a catcher at this time?

227
00:11:31,056 --> 00:11:31,296
[SPEAKER_01]: Huh?

228
00:11:31,316 --> 00:11:32,917
[SPEAKER_01]: What position would you be playing?

229
00:11:32,977 --> 00:11:34,258
[SPEAKER_00]: I was a catcher all the time.

230
00:11:34,697 --> 00:11:36,358
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I could never try to play anything else.

231
00:11:37,880 --> 00:11:41,522
[SPEAKER_00]: But something happened real funny.

232
00:11:41,542 --> 00:11:45,305
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a little town by the name of Eaton over here north of Muncie.

233
00:11:45,906 --> 00:11:49,949
[SPEAKER_00]: And I used to ride a bicycle from Muncie to Eaton.

234
00:11:50,930 --> 00:11:51,890
[SPEAKER_00]: That's 12 miles.

235
00:11:53,432 --> 00:11:54,813
[SPEAKER_00]: Play a game, they give me a quarter.

236
00:11:56,814 --> 00:12:01,358
[SPEAKER_00]: So another little town farther north of Eaton by the name of Montpelier.

237
00:12:02,725 --> 00:12:07,567
[SPEAKER_00]: They came over there and played us one day and this manager, I don't know what his name was either.

238
00:12:09,068 --> 00:12:11,689
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, why don't you come over to Montpierre and play with us?

239
00:12:13,550 --> 00:12:15,331
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, I'll give you more money than they're giving you.

240
00:12:15,631 --> 00:12:16,851
[SPEAKER_00]: I said, how much will you give me?

241
00:12:17,892 --> 00:12:23,615
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, I'll give you 75 cents and pay your way on the interurban there and back.

242
00:12:24,335 --> 00:12:25,756
[SPEAKER_00]: I said, I'll be over there next Sunday.

243
00:12:29,442 --> 00:12:33,863
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, after that, then I started going to Connersville, and we started making good money.

244
00:12:34,964 --> 00:12:37,324
[SPEAKER_00]: We kept $50 to $75 a game.

245
00:12:38,405 --> 00:12:45,007
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's when the scouts from Cincinnati saw me and wanted me to go away.

246
00:12:45,307 --> 00:12:49,088
[SPEAKER_01]: Any particular scout contact you, or you remember talking to him?

247
00:12:49,288 --> 00:12:57,431
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't remember his name, but... What kind of ballparks would you play in at Montpelier and Connersville?

248
00:12:57,611 --> 00:12:58,611
[SPEAKER_00]: Just rough

249
00:12:59,455 --> 00:13:04,798
[SPEAKER_00]: Just a ballpark, just all skin diamonds, no grass.

250
00:13:04,958 --> 00:13:07,640
[SPEAKER_01]: Any grandstands at all, or fences?

251
00:13:08,100 --> 00:13:09,581
[SPEAKER_00]: Just a little grandstand.

252
00:13:10,602 --> 00:13:12,403
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, we had a fence here in Muncie.

253
00:13:13,524 --> 00:13:15,125
[SPEAKER_01]: Didn't you have any artificial turf?

254
00:13:15,765 --> 00:13:17,086
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh no, my land, no.

255
00:13:17,106 --> 00:13:17,786
[SPEAKER_00]: We had mud.

256
00:13:19,007 --> 00:13:19,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Real mud, huh?

257
00:13:19,667 --> 00:13:21,649
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

258
00:13:22,149 --> 00:13:26,071
[SPEAKER_01]: I oftentimes wonder what the old fellas must think when they see that artificial turf.

259
00:13:26,171 --> 00:13:28,773
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh no, we played on, I mean it was rough.

260
00:13:30,695 --> 00:13:34,436
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know how infielders come up with the balls half the time.

261
00:13:35,457 --> 00:13:40,198
[SPEAKER_00]: They really took bad odds.

262
00:13:41,098 --> 00:13:42,279
[SPEAKER_00]: That was the starting of it.

263
00:13:43,359 --> 00:13:47,560
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you actually got started in professional ball at a later age, didn't you?

264
00:13:47,920 --> 00:13:52,262
[SPEAKER_00]: I was 25 years old when the White Sox bought me.

265
00:13:52,522 --> 00:13:52,622
[SPEAKER_00]: 25.

266
00:13:52,922 --> 00:13:54,242
[SPEAKER_00]: That's kind of late.

267
00:13:54,462 --> 00:13:55,043
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

268
00:13:56,063 --> 00:13:59,944
[SPEAKER_00]: And I was the only catcher at that time that ever went out of them.

269
00:14:00,260 --> 00:14:02,282
[SPEAKER_00]: class B league and stayed in the big leagues.

270
00:14:03,143 --> 00:14:04,344
[SPEAKER_00]: I never was farmed back out.

271
00:14:04,404 --> 00:14:05,265
[SPEAKER_00]: I stayed right up there.

272
00:14:05,285 --> 00:14:10,891
[SPEAKER_01]: Were there any other fellows who made it big who were in the central league when you were with Muskegon, do you remember?

273
00:14:11,312 --> 00:14:11,993
[SPEAKER_01]: Any of the teams?

274
00:14:12,153 --> 00:14:14,135
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, Bud Clancy was one.

275
00:14:20,363 --> 00:14:23,046
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, he's the only one I know of in that old Central Lake.

276
00:14:24,327 --> 00:14:27,710
[SPEAKER_01]: Now tell me, what was your feeling when you got to Chicago?

277
00:14:27,750 --> 00:14:30,492
[SPEAKER_01]: You're a big time... Well, I'll tell you.

278
00:14:31,793 --> 00:14:33,555
[SPEAKER_00]: I was dumbfounded, to tell you the truth.

279
00:14:33,655 --> 00:14:35,196
[SPEAKER_00]: Have you been to a big city before?

280
00:14:35,256 --> 00:14:37,959
[SPEAKER_00]: Never been, never been to a big city before in my life.

281
00:14:38,519 --> 00:14:43,023
[SPEAKER_00]: I got off of the boat in Chicago about six o'clock in the morning.

282
00:14:45,546 --> 00:14:46,967
[SPEAKER_00]: And I hadn't had breakfast yet.

283
00:14:47,727 --> 00:14:50,790
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't know where to go or what to do.

284
00:14:51,450 --> 00:14:56,154
[SPEAKER_00]: I got a cab and told them to take me out to the ballpark.

285
00:14:56,194 --> 00:14:58,936
[SPEAKER_00]: I sat out at the ballpark until they opened the gates.

286
00:15:01,598 --> 00:15:02,919
[SPEAKER_01]: I went in and told them who I was.

287
00:15:02,939 --> 00:15:04,840
[SPEAKER_01]: Who did you first report to?

288
00:15:05,521 --> 00:15:06,862
[SPEAKER_00]: Kid Gleason was the manager.

289
00:15:08,303 --> 00:15:10,285
[SPEAKER_00]: What did he say?

290
00:15:11,746 --> 00:15:17,050
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the first thing, I had one of them great big green gloves that catchers use.

291
00:15:17,776 --> 00:15:19,938
[SPEAKER_00]: That is, we used, same pro players used.

292
00:15:21,058 --> 00:15:25,461
[SPEAKER_00]: Just like a pillow, ball head just go poof, no noise or no nothing.

293
00:15:26,482 --> 00:15:27,983
[SPEAKER_00]: Had cheap ball shoes on.

294
00:15:28,283 --> 00:15:30,564
[SPEAKER_00]: When you get them wet, they'd stretch about that big.

295
00:15:30,584 --> 00:15:31,905
[SPEAKER_01]: They brought a uniform with you?

296
00:15:32,866 --> 00:15:34,347
[SPEAKER_00]: No, they didn't take my uniform.

297
00:15:35,007 --> 00:15:36,688
[SPEAKER_00]: I took my shoes and gloves.

298
00:15:38,129 --> 00:15:47,235
[SPEAKER_00]: And I never will forget that, because the first day out, Kid Gleason, the manager of White's house, he said, let me see that glove.

299
00:15:48,307 --> 00:16:01,239
[SPEAKER_00]: after we'd taken our workout, had me out there throwing around the bases, he said, let me see that glove, and I handed him my glove, and he took it and threw it up in the grandstand.

300
00:16:02,019 --> 00:16:04,401
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, you're in the big leagues now, you gotta have some tools.

301
00:16:05,482 --> 00:16:06,743
[SPEAKER_00]: That liked to scare me to death.

302
00:16:08,785 --> 00:16:15,131
[SPEAKER_00]: And when I get up in the clubhouse, I had two brand new short-model catching gloves,

303
00:16:16,152 --> 00:16:23,697
[SPEAKER_00]: And a guy from Spalding fitted me with a pair of featherweight shoes, which I didn't know what a featherweight shoe was in my life.

304
00:16:23,717 --> 00:16:24,817
[SPEAKER_00]: What kind of gloves are those?

305
00:16:25,038 --> 00:16:25,398
[SPEAKER_00]: Shock?

306
00:16:26,158 --> 00:16:26,799
[SPEAKER_00]: Ray Shock.

307
00:16:27,359 --> 00:16:27,979
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, Ray Shock.

308
00:16:27,999 --> 00:16:28,920
[SPEAKER_00]: Bicep catcher.

309
00:16:29,661 --> 00:16:31,041
[SPEAKER_00]: He had a glove model there.

310
00:16:32,582 --> 00:16:35,084
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, I used that glove all the way through my career.

311
00:16:36,205 --> 00:16:37,906
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, that model glove.

312
00:16:39,307 --> 00:16:43,209
[SPEAKER_00]: But, uh, they took pretty good care of me, but he scared me to death.

313
00:16:43,229 --> 00:16:44,550
[SPEAKER_01]: What are these feather shoes?

314
00:16:45,503 --> 00:16:47,924
[SPEAKER_00]: Real just like kid gloves on your feet.

315
00:16:49,185 --> 00:16:51,247
[SPEAKER_00]: Was this a special advantage to a catcher?

316
00:16:51,467 --> 00:16:52,708
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, no.

317
00:16:52,908 --> 00:16:54,870
[SPEAKER_00]: Everybody used them.

318
00:16:54,910 --> 00:16:56,151
[SPEAKER_00]: Make you run faster?

319
00:16:56,171 --> 00:16:57,692
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I could run like a deer.

320
00:16:58,192 --> 00:17:00,554
[SPEAKER_00]: I put them things on, I just felt like I was flying.

321
00:17:01,835 --> 00:17:04,017
[SPEAKER_01]: They hadn't gotten down in the minor leagues with those yet, had they?

322
00:17:04,197 --> 00:17:04,557
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, no.

323
00:17:04,597 --> 00:17:05,378
[SPEAKER_00]: My land, no.

324
00:17:06,707 --> 00:17:08,408
[SPEAKER_00]: A minor league player couldn't afford to buy it.

325
00:17:09,348 --> 00:17:10,849
[SPEAKER_00]: They were putting their $22 in them days.

326
00:17:10,869 --> 00:17:13,390
[SPEAKER_01]: You mean you had to buy your shoes in Muskegon?

327
00:17:13,410 --> 00:17:14,070
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh yeah.

328
00:17:14,770 --> 00:17:15,671
[SPEAKER_00]: We had to buy everything.

329
00:17:16,571 --> 00:17:17,411
[SPEAKER_01]: Not the uniform.

330
00:17:17,972 --> 00:17:19,032
[SPEAKER_00]: No, we didn't buy the uniform.

331
00:17:19,072 --> 00:17:20,452
[SPEAKER_00]: But the other equipment.

332
00:17:20,492 --> 00:17:21,433
[SPEAKER_00]: Shoes and gloves.

333
00:17:22,233 --> 00:17:26,175
[SPEAKER_00]: Gloves and shoes and sweatshirts and stuff like that we had to buy.

334
00:17:26,195 --> 00:17:27,675
[SPEAKER_00]: How'd you travel in the Central League?

335
00:17:28,576 --> 00:17:29,996
[SPEAKER_00]: In the old Central League on a bus.

336
00:17:31,557 --> 00:17:32,317
[SPEAKER_00]: And inter-urban.

337
00:17:33,573 --> 00:17:34,694
[SPEAKER_01]: We traveled in there.

338
00:17:34,814 --> 00:17:38,417
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you make any overnight stops or did you come back home?

339
00:17:39,959 --> 00:17:41,820
[SPEAKER_00]: No, we made overnight stops.

340
00:17:41,840 --> 00:17:42,761
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, we'd stay.

341
00:17:43,642 --> 00:17:45,163
[SPEAKER_00]: Where would you stay overnight?

342
00:17:45,203 --> 00:17:47,085
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes we'd stay two or three days.

343
00:17:48,866 --> 00:17:51,929
[SPEAKER_00]: We rode in urban when we could.

344
00:17:53,751 --> 00:17:55,412
[SPEAKER_00]: I like we'd go from bus gigging

345
00:17:57,742 --> 00:18:00,903
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, we'd go from Muskegon to Hamlin, Ontario.

346
00:18:01,983 --> 00:18:03,044
[SPEAKER_00]: We'd ride a train.

347
00:18:03,924 --> 00:18:07,265
[SPEAKER_00]: If we'd go from Muskegon to Grand Rapids, Michigan, we'd ride in urban.

348
00:18:08,265 --> 00:18:11,546
[SPEAKER_00]: If we'd go to some of them other towns a little closer by, we'd ride a bus.

349
00:18:12,146 --> 00:18:14,867
[SPEAKER_00]: Just according to, I suppose, the cheapest way to go.

350
00:18:15,487 --> 00:18:17,328
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

351
00:18:21,129 --> 00:18:23,990
[SPEAKER_01]: What kind of clubhouse facilities did you have in Central Lake?

352
00:18:25,863 --> 00:18:28,065
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, nothing to brag about.

353
00:18:28,105 --> 00:18:30,427
[SPEAKER_00]: It was just an old wooden locker.

354
00:18:32,409 --> 00:18:35,471
[SPEAKER_00]: And just an old shower where you walk in.

355
00:18:36,412 --> 00:18:41,817
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing like the big leagues where they got nice showers, nice locker room.

356
00:18:42,858 --> 00:18:45,500
[SPEAKER_00]: How much expense money did you get in Muskegon?

357
00:18:47,802 --> 00:18:49,303
[SPEAKER_00]: We didn't get any expense money.

358
00:18:50,224 --> 00:18:50,904
[SPEAKER_00]: They'd make us eat

359
00:18:53,390 --> 00:18:55,571
[SPEAKER_00]: hamburger joint.

360
00:18:55,831 --> 00:18:56,531
[SPEAKER_00]: They'd take us.

361
00:18:57,772 --> 00:18:58,932
[SPEAKER_01]: We didn't get no expense money.

362
00:18:58,952 --> 00:19:04,395
[SPEAKER_01]: They'd take the whole team together, eat in one place, and then they'd pay the bill themselves.

363
00:19:04,955 --> 00:19:07,896
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me check and see how we're coming through here.

364
00:19:09,457 --> 00:19:14,239
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, now back to the White Sox.

365
00:19:15,719 --> 00:19:17,180
[SPEAKER_01]: How was Kit Gleason to you?

366
00:19:17,700 --> 00:19:19,941
[SPEAKER_00]: Kit Gleason was a real, real fellow.

367
00:19:21,141 --> 00:19:22,542
[SPEAKER_00]: I played, let's see, I played under

368
00:19:25,071 --> 00:19:27,772
[SPEAKER_00]: Kid Gleason only stayed there that one year, 23.

369
00:19:29,753 --> 00:19:31,994
[SPEAKER_00]: And they give the job to Frank Chance.

370
00:19:33,415 --> 00:19:36,617
[SPEAKER_00]: And he died while we was in spring training.

371
00:19:36,677 --> 00:19:37,037
[SPEAKER_00]: In 24.

372
00:19:37,077 --> 00:19:39,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Pneumonia.

373
00:19:40,099 --> 00:19:41,159
[SPEAKER_00]: In the spring of 24?

374
00:19:41,940 --> 00:19:43,060
[SPEAKER_00]: In the spring of 24.

375
00:19:44,081 --> 00:19:48,223
[SPEAKER_00]: And then they made Johnny Evers, who was their coach, they made him the manager.

376
00:19:49,564 --> 00:19:52,145
[SPEAKER_00]: So I played under Evers in 24 and

377
00:19:53,825 --> 00:19:54,646
[SPEAKER_00]: Twenty-five.

378
00:19:58,328 --> 00:20:01,130
[SPEAKER_00]: Twenty-five, they give it to... Eddie Collins, isn't it?

379
00:20:01,870 --> 00:20:02,631
[SPEAKER_00]: Eddie Collins.

380
00:20:04,532 --> 00:20:09,135
[SPEAKER_00]: Then I think Eddie Collins had it one, maybe two years.

381
00:20:09,776 --> 00:20:12,418
[SPEAKER_00]: And then they give it to Lena Blackburn.

382
00:20:14,499 --> 00:20:20,203
[SPEAKER_00]: And then Lena, they let Lena go after a year.

383
00:20:22,021 --> 00:20:24,442
[SPEAKER_00]: I believe it was a year, and then they give it to Ray Shock.

384
00:20:25,843 --> 00:20:29,545
[SPEAKER_00]: Ray Shock managed them for two years.

385
00:20:29,966 --> 00:20:33,488
[SPEAKER_01]: Wasn't this demoralizing, having so many changes in the managers?

386
00:20:33,508 --> 00:20:34,368
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it wasn't good.

387
00:20:35,809 --> 00:20:39,592
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, you didn't know whether he was going or coming.

388
00:20:42,213 --> 00:20:45,595
[SPEAKER_00]: And from Ray Shock, then they give it to Oney Bush.

389
00:20:48,022 --> 00:20:48,823
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, only Bush.

390
00:20:49,864 --> 00:20:50,745
[SPEAKER_00]: He was the last one.

391
00:20:52,126 --> 00:20:53,367
[SPEAKER_01]: Who was the best of all those?

392
00:20:53,707 --> 00:20:54,828
[SPEAKER_01]: Or couldn't you pick one up?

393
00:20:56,209 --> 00:20:59,853
[SPEAKER_01]: Any standout of knowing the baseball best or being the best manager?

394
00:21:00,133 --> 00:21:00,793
[SPEAKER_00]: Eddie Collins.

395
00:21:01,614 --> 00:21:06,238
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, I didn't play for Gleason long enough to really know about him.

396
00:21:08,180 --> 00:21:10,402
[SPEAKER_00]: But Eddie Collins was the best.

397
00:21:11,223 --> 00:21:12,424
[SPEAKER_00]: How about Evers?

398
00:21:12,724 --> 00:21:14,405
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, he was a radical guy.

399
00:21:15,285 --> 00:21:15,565
[SPEAKER_00]: A what?

400
00:21:15,985 --> 00:21:17,826
[SPEAKER_00]: He was a radical sort of a fellow.

401
00:21:18,086 --> 00:21:18,667
[SPEAKER_00]: In what way?

402
00:21:19,027 --> 00:21:23,849
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, he's one of these guys who hide behind a tree to see if he's coming in at midnight.

403
00:21:24,089 --> 00:21:25,210
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, is that right?

404
00:21:26,670 --> 00:21:27,010
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

405
00:21:27,050 --> 00:21:28,311
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, he was rough.

406
00:21:29,432 --> 00:21:30,392
[SPEAKER_00]: He was a rough guy.

407
00:21:30,652 --> 00:21:34,554
[SPEAKER_02]: Who was the most lenient of them all?

408
00:21:34,594 --> 00:21:36,135
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I'd say Ray Sharp was.

409
00:21:37,556 --> 00:21:41,277
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you think a manager can be pretty lenient and still be a real good manager?

410
00:21:41,477 --> 00:21:42,457
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

411
00:21:42,877 --> 00:21:44,718
[SPEAKER_01]: But he's got to have the respect of his players.

412
00:21:45,098 --> 00:21:45,938
[SPEAKER_00]: How does he get that?

413
00:21:46,338 --> 00:21:47,139
[SPEAKER_00]: By being lenient?

414
00:21:48,059 --> 00:21:50,640
[SPEAKER_00]: By just being, just being one of the boys.

415
00:21:52,380 --> 00:21:53,220
[SPEAKER_00]: Was Sharp that way?

416
00:21:53,320 --> 00:21:53,640
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

